<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kayseri</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Caesarea Mazaca, Mazaca</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kayseri is a city in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, at the foot of the extinct volcano Erciyes. As Caesarea Mazaca it was the capital of the kingdom of Cappadocia and the see of Basil the Great. Taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danishmendids">Danishmendids</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> of Rum, it gained many mosques, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a>, and it remains a major commercial centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kayseri.jpg" alt="Kayseri" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Erciyes_(Argaeus),_Turkey_(31870998063).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The city lies on a low spur at the foot of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-erciyes">Mount Erciyes</a>, a dormant volcano that rises to 3,917 metres, more than 1,500 metres above the city's mean altitude. In antiquity the mountain was Mount Argaeus. The site stood at a crossing of trade routes: that from Sinope to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, and the Persian Royal Road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> during more than two centuries of Achaemenid rule. In Roman times a route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> to the east crossed it as well.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Kayseri has been equated with the early Hittite kingdom of Kussara, mentioned in early Assyrian trading records, and a first period of prosperity is dated to about 2000 BCE, when it served as a trading post between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a>. As Mazaca it was the residence of the kings of Cappadocia. According to Armenian tradition it was founded by Mishak. In 14 CE the last king, Archelaus, renamed it Caesarea in Cappadocia in honour of Augustus. In the fourth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-of-caesarea">Basil of Caesarea</a> founded there a large complex of charitable institutions, a monastery and churches, known as the Basiliad.</p><p>From the mid-seventh century Arab attacks on Cappadocia became common and the city was besieged several times; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muawiya-i">Muawiyah</a> took it temporarily in 647. The Seljuk ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alp-arslan">Alp Arslan</a> demolished the city and massacred its population in 1067, and it stood uninhabited for about half a century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danishmendids">Danishmendids</a> rebuilt it in 1134. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a>, from 1178 to 1243, it was one of the sultanate's most important centres, until it fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> in 1243. In 1277 Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a>, after his victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbistan">Elbistan</a>, entered the city and massacred its Christians while sparing the Muslim inhabitants. The Eretnids held it in the fourteenth century and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took it in 1515.</p><p>The Seljuk period left the Hunat Hatun Complex, the Kiliç Arslan Mosque, the Grand Mosque and the Gevher Nesibe hospital. The Grand Mosque was begun by the Danishmend emir Melik Mehmed Gazi and finished by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkish-war-of-independence">Turkish War of Independence</a> the building of the Kayseri Lyceum housed the Turkish Grand National Assembly while the Greek army advanced on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kayseri is the capital of Kayseri province in Central Anatolia. Its huge basalt walls, dating to the reign of Justinian in the sixth century, were repeatedly repaired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and later authorities. The suburb of Talas was the ancestral home of Calouste Gulbenkian, Aristotle Onassis and Elia Kazan, and the architect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mimar-sinan">Mimar Sinan</a> was born in 1490 at Ağırnas nearby. The archaeological site of Kanesh-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanesh">Kültepe</a> lies 20 km to the north-east. Public investment in the 1920s and 1930s brought a textile factory and an aircraft factory to the city, and its merchants later built an industrial base.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kayseri” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Towns such as Konya, <b>Kayseri</b>, Sivas, Aksaray and Malatya grew as centres of crafts and commerce, and the guild-like brotherhoods of the akhis organised urban artisans.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Assyrian merchants of the early 2nd millennium BCE carried it to Anatolia, where some 23,000 of their letters and contracts have been found at Kanesh, near present-day <b>Kayseri</b> in Turkey.</blockquote><a href="/language/akkadian">Akkadian · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>They built mosques and madrasas in Tokat, Niksar and <b>Kayseri</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/danishmendids">Danishmendids</a></li><li><blockquote>His realm included Sivas, <b>Kayseri</b>, Tokat, Amasya and Erzincan, with a Mongol and Turkmen military elite and Persian administration in the Seljuk tradition.</blockquote><a href="/state/eretnids">Eretnids</a></li><li><blockquote>Kadi Burhan al-Din Ahmad was a jurist from <b>Kayseri</b> who served the Eretnids as judge and vizier.</blockquote><a href="/state/kadi-burhan-al-din-state">Kadi Burhan al-Din state</a></li><li><blockquote>His adopted son Ariarathes II regained the land c. 301 BCE, and his heirs took the title king, ruling the plateau from <b>Mazaca</b> beneath Mount Argaeus.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-cappadocia">Kingdom of Cappadocia</a></li></ul></section>
